 ### [Neural Rendering of Variable Scenes](12734-neural-rendering-of-variable-scenes.md)
-Neural rendering of variable scenes is a method that explicitly incorporates scene variation into the training phase by encoding predefined ranges of dynamic scene elements into a structured vector representation. Unlike methods that constrain learning to a single static manifold, these approaches learn a surface radiance field capable of handling all possible permutations of the specified scene variables. This distinguishes them from sibling methods like [DyNeRF](7274-dynerf.md), which typically model deformation or motion through explicit geometric transformations or temporal bases, whereas variable scene methods treat dynamic parameters as direct inputs to the neural field to capture higher-order interactions between moving objects and the static environment.
