 ## Background
-The concept of dynamic scene representation emerged as a natural extension of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) to handle video data and non-static environments. Early works like DyNeRF [S1] demonstrated that a single MLP could model temporal variations by concatenating time embeddings with spatial coordinates. Subsequent research focused on improving efficiency and detail preservation, leading to explicit representations like 4D Gaussian Splatting and deformation fields like GauFRe. These methods address the limitations of implicit neural networks, such as high-frequency detail loss and memory inefficiency, by leveraging explicit primitives and temporal interpolation. The field continues to evolve with a focus on real-time rendering and memory-efficient representations for applications like AR/VR and dynamic novel view synthesis.
+The concept of representing dynamic scenes using neural radiance fields emerged with the need to extend static NeRF capabilities to video and dynamic content. Early works like DyNeRF established the baseline of using temporal embeddings in shared MLPs [S1]. Subsequent research focused on efficiency and explicit modeling, leading to methods like Ex4DGS and GauFRe which leverage Gaussian Splatting for faster rendering and explicit temporal control [S1]. The field continues to evolve with compression techniques like LGS to handle resource-constrained environments [S1].
 
